# MonoMod reconstruction provenance.
# Consumed by scripts/build_monomod.sh.
#
# MonoMod is not optional on Android, and the reason is a single line of
# upstream code. Official MonoMod/MonoMod, at the pinned commit:
#
#   src/MonoMod.Core/Platforms/PlatformTriple.cs
#     OSKind.Android => throw new NotImplementedException(),
#
# Official MonoMod throws on Android. The patch below is the Android support:
# a 447-line AndroidSystem, an Android interop class (Android's sysconf and
# errno differ from Linux's), and the W^X / mprotect handling that Android's
# memory model requires. Without it every Harmony patch on device fails.
#
# The Android support is not ours and does not need to be: it is published as 8
# commits on top of upstream in the public XtraCube/MonoMod fork, on branch
# origin/reorganize, tip d41aaf9 (2026-08-14, includes upstream merges through 2026-08-05).
#
# The base those commits now sit on, d2f2fd9, is itself an official
# MonoMod/MonoMod commit (origin/reorganize, 2026-08-05). So
# the repository we clone is the official one and the only thing we hold is the
# patch: the same shape as references/il2cppinterop-arm64-disarm.patch. Nothing
# is taken from a dead feed, no fork is vendored, and no nupkg is trusted as an
# opaque input.
#
# Applying the patch to official d2f2fd9 reproduces the fork's tree byte for
# byte (verified by comparing the result against d41aaf9), so the assemblies
# this produces are the Android work as published. The previous base, 20ccecf
# (2025-11-17), was what the Android commits were first written on; d2f2fd9
# takes upstream through 2026-08-05 without the patch having to be
# re-authored, because the fork itself merged upstream up to that point.
UPSTREAM_URL=https://github.com/MonoMod/MonoMod
# The ref the pin is compared against by scripts/check_upstream_pins.sh. Not
# master: master deleted PlatformTriple.cs/Unix.cs/OSKind and the patch would
# need re-authoring. origin/reorganize is the branch the published Android
# work (the Android commits plus two upstream merges on top of d2f2fd9) sits on.
UPSTREAM_REF=reorganize
UPSTREAM_COMMIT=d2f2fd91f0daababe41b81239b3d53a4be77cd43
PATCH_FILE=references/monomod-android.patch

# Recorded for traceability: the fork tip the patch reproduces. Not fetched or
# built; only asserted against, so a patch edit that stops matching it fails.
FORK_URL=https://github.com/XtraCube/MonoMod
FORK_TIP=d41aaf9fd9a552ac5bc16c58701c6ca8c975019d

# Upstream requires an exact SDK that predates the installed one. Its
# global.json pins 10.0.100 with rollForward=latestPatch, which cannot cross a
# major version, so a machine with only 10.0.401 or an 11.0 preview fails
# before compiling anything. CI hides this by installing 10.0.x explicitly; a
# clean local runner does not. The build overrides the pin rather than asking
# the operator to install an SDK version nobody else uses.
SDK_VERSION=10.0.401

# Distinct from the fork's 25.3.4-star / 25.0.12-star / 1.3.4-star so NuGet's
# global cache (keyed by id+version, not by source feed) cannot silently satisfy
# a reference from an unrelated upstream package. This is the same reason
# il2cppinterop.env uses 1.5.2-android.3.
#
# Note what the -star suffix meant: a prerelease label on a fork build. NuGet
# reports it as unresolvable and falls back to the official release with an
# NU1603 warning, which is how a build came to compile against official
# MonoMod - code that throws NotImplementedException on Android - while the
# shipped assemblies were built from the fork. A pin that fails open is worse
# than one that fails.
# Named distinctly from il2cppinterop.env's PACKAGE_VERSION because both files
# are sourced into the same shell by scripts/package_bepinex_android.sh; a
# shared name would silently make one pin overwrite the other.
MONOMOD_PACKAGE_VERSION=25.3.7-android.1
LOCAL_FEED=local-nuget

# The three packages BepInEx.Unity.IL2CPP resolves. Every one of them is
# multi-targeted upstream (net35 through net10.0 plus netstandard2.0), so a
# "same name" comparison proves nothing: the packaging gate compares the
# net10.0 asset specifically, because that is the one CoreCLR loads.
#
# These TFMs were read off the device-tested assemblies, not guessed: all three
# shipped MonoMod.Core/Utils/RuntimeDetour.dll carry
# TargetFrameworkAttribute(".NETCoreApp,Version=v10.0").
# One "name tfm" pair per line, not space-separated on one line: the build
# script reads these line by line, and a single-line list would be split into
# bare words.
MONOMOD_COMPONENTS='Core net10.0
Utils net10.0
RuntimeDetour net10.0'